BITwiki, BIThub, agent systems, embeddings or local semantic architecture may appear only in explicitly labeled applications after the general concept is established.

== Computational-semantics boundary ==

Wave 3 deliberately includes both linguistic theory and computational models. The bridge must preserve the distinction:

<pre>
observed language use
      ↓
corpus / annotations / lexical resources
      ↓
distributional or symbolic representation
      ↓
similarity / parsing / inference / downstream task

model output ≠ automatically linguistic meaning
statistical association ≠ reference
embedding proximity ≠ identity
benchmark agreement ≠ semantic truth
</pre>

[[Distributional semantics]], [[Semantic similarity]] and [[Meaning representation]] therefore need explicit epistemic/measurement limitations.

== Title and identity risks ==

* <code>Discourse</code> is unavailable for the linguistic concept because it already denotes the forum software. Use provisional [[Discourse (linguistics)]].
* <code>Context</code> is extremely broad. Capture currently supports a general article centered on interpretive context with explicit linguistic/pragmatic senses, but Phase 2 must check whether a disambiguated <code>Context (linguistics)</code> title is safer.
* <code>Reference</code> is broader than language. Keep the concept general enough to cover representational reference, with linguistic reference as the principal case, rather than silently reducing the title to one theory.
* <code>Meaning representation</code> is a computational/formal term and should not be presented as a synonym for mental representation or semantic content.
* <code>Semantic similarity</code> must separate similarity, relatedness, association and identity.

== Controlled-Domain issue ==

Current <code>bitwiki-runtime-schema.json</code> Domains are:

<pre>
Systems science
Science
Biology
Computer science
Mathematics
Philosophy
Technology
Electronics
Energy
Engineering
Chemistry
Physics
Medicine
</pre>

No <code>Linguistics</code> Domain exists.

The majority of Wave 3 concepts are foundational linguistics topics. Assigning every page to <code>Philosophy</code> would misrepresent empirical linguistics; assigning computational descendants to <code>Computer science</code> alone would erase their linguistic identity.

'''Phase-1 architectural requirement:''' decide whether to add controlled Domain <code>Linguistics</code> before Phase 2. If added, compiler schema, runtime projection, categories/portal/navigation and validation must be updated through the existing living-text rule rather than patched locally in article metadata.

== Source-family audit ==

* '''Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy''' — pragmatics, reference, ambiguity, compositionality, presupposition, implicature, speech acts, common ground, philosophy/methodology of linguistics.
* '''Oxford Research Encyclopedia of Linguistics''' — lexical semantics and polysemy.
* '''Cambridge linguistics references''' — syntax, discourse, discourse analysis and broader linguistic framing.
* '''Foundational primary literature''' — Frege, Grice, Austin, Searle, Kaplan, Stalnaker, Lewis, Montague, Heim and related primary works where the article discusses their specific theories.
* '''Computational linguistics / ACL''' — meaning representations, semantic similarity tasks, computational lexical semantics.
* '''Annual Review / JAIR''' — distributional semantics and vector-space semantics.
* '''Jurafsky & Martin''' — current broad computational-linguistics synthesis. The current online 3rd-edition manuscript is dated 19 August 2026 and should be cited as a dated online manuscript, not as a timeless finalized edition.
* '''Wikipedia''' — terminology/discovery/redirect checking only where stronger sources exist.
* '''Scholarpedia''' — use only where a specific article genuinely covers the target concept better than primary/reference literature.

